{
  "preference": "B",
  "reason": "B better supports the director's decision: its 238-word body explicitly calculates the zero-point difference-in-differences and proposes an assignment-based decision rule. A's body exceeds 350 words and adds problematic advice to estimate effects among users from uptake, plus the unsupported assertion that without a preset threshold a randomized test would be 'as uninformative as the first.' Both correctly reject a demonstrated causal improvement; A more explicitly addresses missing earlier trends.",
  "reports": {
    "A": {
      "task_fidelity": {
        "score": 2,
        "reason": "Addresses the requested calculations, design and next test, and cites both sources, but substantially exceeds the 180–260-word limit.",
        "excerpt": "“Recommended next test”: “Randomize the offer of reminders next month.” “Decision criterion”: “Before examining results, management must set the smallest worthwhile improvement in on-time return rate.”",
        "source_evidence": "S2 permits randomizing an offer next month and requires management to set the smallest worthwhile benefit before examining results. The brief requires 180–260 words; A exceeds 350 words before its source footer."
      },
      "facts_and_evidence": {
        "score": 3,
        "reason": "The arithmetic and principal design facts are accurate. However, saying the next randomized test would be equally uninformative without a preset threshold confuses causal information with decision readiness.",
        "excerpt": "“What the numbers show”: “Both groups improved by exactly 5 points.” “Decision criterion”: “Without this preset, the second test will be as uninformative as the first.”",
        "source_evidence": "S1 reports 75→80 and 55→60 out of 100. S2 requires a management-set threshold but does not say its absence eliminates the informational value of randomized assignment."
      },
      "explanation_and_synthesis": {
        "score": 3,
        "reason": "Clearly explains why the unchanged group gap does not establish causation. The uptake-based recommendation lacks the assumptions needed to identify an effect among users.",
        "excerpt": "“What the numbers show”: “The group difference therefore did not widen or narrow.” “Recommended next test”: “separately examine uptake to estimate the effect among those who used reminders.”",
        "source_evidence": "S1 implies a zero-point difference-in-differences. S2 permits randomizing the offer and recording uptake, not randomizing actual use; it supplies no assumptions identifying an effect among users."
      },
      "counterevidence_and_boundaries": {
        "score": 3,
        "reason": "Explicitly considers selection, shared amnesty and missing prior trends. Boundaries are weaker when discussing the future user-effect estimate and when treating the baseline gap as direct evidence of systematic selection differences.",
        "excerpt": "“Why the design cannot support a causal claim”: “Earlier trends and reasons for opting in were not recorded”; “the 20-point baseline gap is direct evidence of such differences.”",
        "source_evidence": "S2 explicitly records voluntary enrollment, missing earlier trends and selection reasons, and amnesty exposure in both groups. S1 establishes a baseline outcome difference, not its mechanism."
      },
      "reader_usefulness": {
        "score": 3,
        "reason": "Provides an actionable randomized-offer test, intention-to-treat comparison and prospective threshold. Excess length and potentially misleading uptake analysis reduce its reliability as a concise decision note.",
        "excerpt": "“Recommended next test”: “Compare on-time return rates between assignment groups (intention-to-treat)”; “Decision criterion”: “Adopt reminders only if the estimated effect reaches that threshold and the confidence interval excludes no benefit.”",
        "source_evidence": "S2 says staff can randomize offers and record assignment, uptake and return status, and management must choose the worthwhile-benefit threshold."
      }
    },
    "B": {
      "task_fidelity": {
        "score": 4,
        "reason": "Meets the requested length and audience, uses source-ID citations, calculates relevant comparisons, distinguishes descriptive from causal conclusions, and proposes a feasible test with a decision criterion.",
        "excerpt": "“Descriptive results”: “The difference-in-differences estimate is therefore 0 percentage points”; “Proposed next test”: “Randomize an offer of reminders next month, recording assignment, uptake, and return status (S2).”",
        "source_evidence": "S1 supplies the four counts and equal group denominators. S2 explicitly authorizes the proposed randomized-offer design. The body is approximately 238 words."
      },
      "facts_and_evidence": {
        "score": 4,
        "reason": "Calculations and cited design facts are accurate. The suggested three-point threshold is clearly an example for management, not a source-derived requirement.",
        "excerpt": "“Descriptive results”: “(+5 percentage points, or +6.7% relative)” and “(+5 points, or +9.1% relative)”; “Proposed next test”: “for example, 3 percentage points”.",
        "source_evidence": "S1 yields 5/75≈6.7% and 5/55≈9.1%, equal five-point gains and unchanged 20-point gaps. S2 states that no smallest worthwhile benefit has been preset."
      },
      "explanation_and_synthesis": {
        "score": 3,
        "reason": "Effectively connects the baseline gap, equal changes and nonrandom enrollment to the causal limitation. It should explicitly explain that the zero difference-in-differences lacks a supported parallel-trends assumption.",
        "excerpt": "“Descriptive results”: “that gap already existed before the pilot”; “Design assessment”: “the 5-point within-group gains cannot be attributed to reminders.”",
        "source_evidence": "S1 establishes the unchanged gap. S2 states that enrollment was voluntary and earlier trends were not recorded, limiting causal interpretation of the change comparison."
      },
      "counterevidence_and_boundaries": {
        "score": 3,
        "reason": "Recognizes selection and concurrent amnesty and rejects demonstrated causation. Omits missing earlier trends and uses 'no reminder effect is detectable' ambiguously: nonidentification is not evidence of no effect.",
        "excerpt": "“Design assessment”: “The appropriate causal claim is that no reminder effect is detectable from this design”.",
        "source_evidence": "S2 reports nonrandom enrollment, unrecorded earlier trends and shared amnesty exposure. These prevent a supported causal conclusion; they do not establish that the true effect is zero."
      },
      "reader_usefulness": {
        "score": 4,
        "reason": "Gives a concise, implementable next step and a prospective decision rule tied to the effect of offering reminders, avoiding an unsupported claim about effects among users.",
        "excerpt": "“Proposed next test”: “If the intention-to-treat effect is at least that threshold and statistically distinguishable from zero, expand the reminder offer; otherwise, do not scale it.”",
        "source_evidence": "S2 permits randomized offers with assignment, uptake and outcome recording and requires management to set the worthwhile-benefit threshold before examining results."
      }
    }
  },
  "condition_clues": "After judgment: A's closing disclaimer about independent review and provisional assessment is a possible process-related stylistic clue. B uses inline citations and a shorter conventional note format. Neither reliably identifies a condition; these features were not treated as evidence of authorship or tooling."
}
